  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CST, skies are becoming a mix of sun and clouds. Fog has \nburned off/lifted over much of the forecast area, with just a few \npockets of light fog continuing across the Missouri River valley \nregion. Temperatures have been trying to warm through the 30s into \nthe low 40s on southerly winds 5 to 15 mph.\n\nCurrent satellite trends suggest all of the low clouds and fog will \nbe gone by sundown, allowing for a reset of the boundary layer for \nlater tonight. Mainly will be just dealing with some sct-bkn \nmid/high clouds for the first few hours of the tonight period \nforecast. Short term guidance brings the fog back again tonight, \nmainly east river, including the development of fog over into west \ncentral Minnesota prior to sunrise Saturday morning. Southwesterly \nlow level winds (0.5km) tonight do support a bit of a downslope wind \nscenario in the lee of the Prairie Coteau, so expecting to see a \nhole in the fog/low clouds (from subsidence) there in the lee of the \nCoteau, surrounded by fog/low clouds. REFS probabilities for visby \ndown to or less than 0.5mi is in that 50-70 percent range over a \ngood chunk of eastern South Dakota and western Minnesota by sunrise \non Saturday.\n\nNo other notable changes showing up in the 7-day forecast. Still \nlooks dry through next Thursday. Still looks like a warm up this \nweekend into Wednesday of next week. The next strong cold frontal \npassage may be lining up for Wednesday or Wednesday night of next \nweek. Fire Weather-wise, breezy west-northwest winds across the \nnorthern tier counties on Saturday, coupled with afternoon humidity \nvalues dropping to around 40-45 percent is expected to bump \nGrassland Fire Danger indexes up to moderate to high, particularly \nover north central South Dakota.",
